1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,618
Some questions about a codebase take an afternoon to answer.

2
00:00:03,618 --> 00:00:06,650
How does a rating get from a phone, to the big screen?

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:10,251
What if you could just ask?

4
00:00:10,251 --> 00:00:11,965
And then watch the answer happen.

5
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:18,557
This is Ask CrowdPulse.

6
00:00:18,557 --> 00:00:21,966
It runs on your own machine, right next to the code.

7
00:00:21,966 --> 00:00:25,178
Type a question, say who’s asking, and press Ask.

8
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:31,009
Claude Code reads the repository.

9
00:00:31,009 --> 00:00:36,242
A few seconds later, a short answer appears, and then the steps start to arrive.

10
00:00:36,242 --> 00:00:39,645
The animation begins before the answer has finished.

11
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:43,698
Each step is one claim.

12
00:00:43,698 --> 00:00:48,387
Something travels between two parts of the app, and the caption says what it is.

13
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:54,570
Phone, to connection, to database.

14
00:00:54,570 --> 00:00:57,641
Then back out, on a broadcast, to every screen.

15
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:03,425
Here’s why you can trust it.

16
00:01:03,425 --> 00:01:05,788
Every step quotes a line of the real code,

17
00:01:05,788 --> 00:01:09,445
and that quote is looked up in the file before the step is shown.

18
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,334
To show what happens when the model gets it wrong,

19
00:01:16,334 --> 00:01:18,429
we planted two mistakes in this answer.

20
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:25,151
This step jumps between two parts that aren’t connected on the map.

21
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:26,698
It never reaches the screen.

22
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:33,765
And this one quotes a line that isn’t in the file.

23
00:01:33,765 --> 00:01:36,133
It’s shown, but flagged as unverified.

24
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:42,296
A verified step proves the line exists.

25
00:01:42,296 --> 00:01:45,934
Whether the caption reads it correctly is still your call,

26
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:48,255
so the line is always one click away.

27
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:52,558
Under the hood, the map is fixed.

28
00:01:52,558 --> 00:01:55,970
Eighteen parts of the app and the connections between them,

29
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:58,053
each one anchored to a line of code.

30
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:03,596
Claude doesn’t draw anything.

31
00:02:03,596 --> 00:02:08,234
It gets the map and an outline of the code, and replies with a list of steps,

32
00:02:08,234 --> 00:02:09,018
one per line.

33
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:15,575
Because the map is already on screen,

34
00:02:15,575 --> 00:02:19,244
the answer can start playing as soon as the first line lands.

35
00:02:19,244 --> 00:02:21,350
Here, that took about four seconds.

36
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:26,704
The same question can be asked three ways.

37
00:02:26,704 --> 00:02:28,651
A developer gets function names.

38
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:35,025
An architect gets boundaries and trade-offs.

39
00:02:35,025 --> 00:02:37,214
A product owner gets plain language.

40
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,753
Same map, same code, three different stories.

41
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:49,549
The prepared examples double as tests.

42
00:02:49,549 --> 00:02:51,632
Every one has to play on the map,

43
00:02:51,632 --> 00:02:54,979
and every quoted line has to exist in the code today.

44
00:02:57,950 --> 00:03:03,340
Change a line that an answer depends on, and the test run fails and names the quote.

45
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:05,907
The explanations can’t quietly go stale.

46
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:09,997
Ask CrowdPulse.

47
00:03:09,997 --> 00:03:11,045
Ask a question.

48
00:03:11,045 --> 00:03:12,233
Watch the answer.

49
00:03:12,233 --> 00:03:13,280
Check the line.

50
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:23,011
To try it, run npm run ask, and open localhost, port thirty-one hundred.
